3.3..., not divisible\n- 397/19 = 20.9..., not divisible\n\nSo 397 is prime.\n\nNow let me think about this recurrence. We have $a_{n+1} \\equiv a_n(a_n+1)^{-2} \\pmod{397}$.\n\nLet me try a substitution. Let $b_n = \\frac{a_n}{a_n + 1}$. Then:\n\n$b_{n+1} = \\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_{n+1} + 1}$\n\nWe know $a_{n+1}(a_n+1)^2 = a_n$, so $a_{n+1} = \\frac{a_n}{(a_n+1)^2}$.\n\nThen $a_{n+1} + 1 = \\frac{a_n}{(a_n+1)^2} + 1 = \\frac{a_n + (a_n+1)^2}{(a_n+1)^2} = \\frac{a_n + a_n^2 + 2a_n + 1}{(a_n+1)^2} = \\frac{a_n^2 + 3a_n + 1}{(a_n+1)^2}$.\n\nSo $b_{n+1} = \\frac{a_n/(a_n+1)^2}{(a_n^2+3a_n+1)/(a_n+1)^2} = \\frac{a_n}{a_n^2+3a_n+1} = \\frac{1}{a_n + 3 + 1/a_n}$.\n\nHmm, that's not as clean. Let me try another substitution.\n\nLet me try $b_n = \\frac{1}{a_n}$. Then $a_n = 1/b_n$ and $a_n + 1 = (1+b_n)/b_n$.\n\n$a_{n+1}(a_n+1)^2 = a_n$\n$a_{n+1} \\cdot \\frac{(1+b_n)^2}{b_n^2} = \\frac{1}{b_n}$\n$a_{n+1} = \\frac{b_n}{(1+b_n)^2}$\n$\\frac{1}{b_{n+1}} = \\frac{b_n}{(1+b_n)^2}$\n$b_{n+1} = \\frac{(1+b_n)^2}{b_n} = \\frac{1 + 2b_n + b_n^2}{b_n} = \\frac{1}{b_n} + 2 + b_n$\n\nSo $b_{n+1} = b_n + 2 + \\frac{1}{b_n}$.\n\nHmm, that's still not the simplest. Let me try yet another approach.\n\nActually, let me try $c_n = a_n + 1$. Then $a_n = c_n - 1$ and:\n$a_{n+1} \\cdot c_n^2 = c_n - 1$\n$a_{n+1} = \\frac{c_n - 1}{c_n^2}$\n$c_{n+1} = a_{n+1} + 1 = \\frac{c_n - 1}{c_n^2} + 1 = \\frac{c_n - 1 + c_n^2}{c_n^2} = \\frac{c_n^2 + c_n - 1}{c_n^2}$\n\nSo $c_{n+1} = 1 + \\frac{1}{c_n} - \\frac{1}{c_n^2}$.\n\nHmm, let me try another substitution. Let me think about what kind of transformation would linearize this.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Let me try $d_n = \\frac{a_n + 1}{a_n} = 1 + \\frac{1}{a_n} = b_n + 1$ where $b_n = 1/a_n$.\n\nFrom $b_{n+1} = b_n + 2 + 1/b_n$, we get $d_{n+1} = b_{n+1} + 1 = b_n + 3 + 1/b_n = (d_n - 1) + 3 + 1/(d_n-1) = d_n + 2 + \\frac{1}{d_n-1}$.\n\nThat's not simpler either.\n\nLet me try a different approach. What if I think about this in terms of the multiplicative group $\\mathbb{Z}_{397}^*$?\n\nWe have $a_{n+1} \\equiv a_n(a_n+1)^{-2} \\pmod{397}$.\n\nLet me compute a few terms to see if there's a pattern.\n\n$a_0 = 1$\n$a_1 \\cdot (1+1)^2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{397}$\n$a_1 \\cdot 4 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{397}$\n$a_1 \\equiv 4^{-1} \\pmod{397}$\n\n$4 \\cdot 99 = 396 \\equiv -1$, so $4 \\cdot 298 = 1192$. $1192 = 3 \\cdot 397 + 1 = 1191 + 1$. So $4^{-1} \\equiv 298 \\pmod{397}$.\n\nActually, $4 \\cdot 298 = 1192$. $1192 / 397 = 3$ remainder $1192 - 1191 = 1$. Yes, $4^{-1} = 298$.\n\nSo $a_1 = 298$.\n\n$a_2 \\cdot (298+1)^2 \\equiv 298 \\pmod{397}$\n$298 + 1 = 299$\n$299^2 = 89401$\n$89401 / 397$: $397 \\cdot 225 = 89325$. $89401 - 89325 = 76$.\nSo $299^2 \\equiv 76 \\pmod{397}$.\n\n$a_2 \\cdot 76 \\equiv 298 \\pmod{397}$\n$a_2 \\equiv 298 \\cdot 76^{-1} \\pmod{397}$\n\n$76^{-1} \\pmod{397}$: Using extended Euclidean algorithm.\n$397 = 5 \\cdot 76 + 17$\n$76 = 4 \\cdot 17 + 8$\n$17 = 2 \\cdot 8 + 1$\n$8 = 8 \\cdot 1$\n\nBack-substituting:\n$1 = 17 - 2 \\cdot 8 = 17 - 2(76 - 4 \\cdot 17) = 9 \\cdot 17 - 2 \\cdot 76 = 9(397 - 5 \\cdot 76) - 2 \\cdot 76 = 9 \\cdot 397 - 47 \\cdot 76$\n\nSo $76^{-1} \\equiv -47 \\equiv 350 \\pmod{397}$.\n\n$a_2 \\equiv 298 \\cdot 350 \\pmod{397}$\n$298 \\cdot 350 = 104300$\n$104300 / 397$: $397 \\cdot 262 = 104014$. $104300 - 104014 = 286$.\nSo $a_2 = 286$.\n\nLet me check: $286 + 1 = 287$. $287^2 = 82369$. $82369 / 397$: $397 \\cdot 207 = 82179$. $82369 - 82179 = 190$.\n$a_3 \\cdot 190 \\equiv 286 \\pmod{397}$.\n$190^{-1} \\pmod{397}$:\n$397 = 2 \\cdot 190 + 17$\n$190 = 11 \\cdot 17 + 3$\n$17 = 5 \\cdot 3 + 2$\n$3 = 1 \\cdot 2 + 1$\n\n$1 = 3 - 2 = 3 - (17 - 5 \\cdot 3) = 6 \\cdot 3 - 17 = 6(190 - 11 \\cdot 17) - 17 = 6 \\cdot 190 - 67 \\cdot 17 = 6 \\cdot 190 - 67(397 - 2 \\cdot 190) = 140 \\cdot 190 - 67 \\cdot 397$\n\nSo $190^{-1} \\equiv 140 \\pmod{397}$.\n\n$a_3 \\equiv 286 \\cdot 140 = 40040$. $40040 / 397$: $397 \\cdot 100 = 39700$. $40040 - 39700 = 340$.\nSo $a_3 = 340$.\n\nLet me check: $340 + 